 "title": "Amateur Astronomy Targets",
 "source": "Positions, magnitudes and sizes are rounded catalogue values (Messier/NGC), good to about 0.1 hour / 1 degree. Core boost is my own rough estimate of how many magnitudes brighter the bright centre is than the whole-object average. Best season is when the target is highest around midnight (by right ascension), with a few hand adjustments. For star clusters and double stars the core uses the magnitude of the brightest member stars (brightStar, approximate) instead of a core boost. The 10 brightest stars in the northern sky (north of the celestial equator) are listed from memory (approximate positions and magnitudes); variable stars use a typical value, and Rigil Kentaurus is the Alpha Centauri system combined. Planets use typical (average) magnitudes and disk sizes, and are scored over the whole year at every position they can be in the sky. Moon uses an average magnitude over its phases, and moonlight is not counted against other targets.",
